Restore the umask after reading it. uuencode read the umask with
umask(0666) for the stdin case and left it set, so a named file was
then stat()ed under a umask of 0666. On a system that derives the
permission bits it reports from the umask, as busybox-w32 does, the
begin line for a writable named file always showed 644, whatever the
umask was, instead of the mode stat() reports for it elsewhere (666
under a umask of 0, for example). The patch reads the umask, puts it
back at once, and computes the stdin mode from the saved value.

---
 coreutils/uuencode.c |    4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/coreutils/uuencode.c
+++ b/coreutils/uuencode.c
@@ -48,7 +48,9 @@
 	char dst_buf[DST_BUF_SIZE + 1];
 
 	tbl = bb_uuenc_tbl_std;
-	mode = 0666 & ~umask(0666);
+	mode = umask(0);
+	umask(mode);
+	mode = 0666 & ~mode;
 	if (getopt32(argv, "^" "m" "\0" "-1:?2"/*must have 1 or 2 args*/)) {
 		tbl = bb_uuenc_tbl_base64;
 	}
